{"id": 63, "ts": 1784188546.770341, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 4, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I respect the emphasis on Candidate\u202fC\u2019s hazard\u2011anticipation and systems\u2011thinking abilities, but the missing evidence on two core safety criteria\u2014stress resilience and decision quality under pressure\u2014remains a decisive gap. We have no observations of C handling high\u2011stress situations or making safety\u2011critical decisions, whereas Candidate\u202fB is explicitly noted to \u201chandle stress very well\u201d and is \u201cvery conscientious,\u201d both essential for long\u2011haul operations. Moreover, C\u2019s perceived \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d attitude and reluctance to new ideas raise red flags for crew cooperation and adaptability, which are just as vital in the confined cockpit environment. Candidate\u202fB\u2019s \u201cnasty remarks\u201d are concerning, yet they are a behavioral issue that can be addressed through training and coaching, whereas the lack of proven stress\u2011resilience and decision\u2011making for C cannot be mitigated post\u2011hire. Therefore, I continue to recommend Candidate\u202fB as the best overall fit."}
